{
  "paraId": "193B42C3",
  "style": "",
  "text": "Our study investigated the outcomes of lung cancer surgery in elderly patients, focusing on postoperative complications and mortality rates. We observed an in-hospital mortality rate of 1.7% after lung cancer surgery. Benker et al. [7] reported an overall operative mortality rate of 1.56%, similar to our finding. In contrast, Detillon et al. [8] found a higher operative mortality rate by 2.1% in patients ≥ 60 years with lung cancer resections compared to our data. In a similar vein, Benker et al. [7] reported an overall complication rate of 38.6% in 1219 patients who underwent anatomical resection for non-small cell lung cancer, comprising pulmonary complications in 26.4%, cardiac complications in 14.8%, and gastrointestinal complications in 3.0%; comorbidities were present in 78.9% of their patients, most commonly hypertension (34.1%), COPD (26.4%), and other malignancies (19%). Their overall morbidity was therefore somewhat higher than the 29.3% rate of Clavien–Dindo grade ≥ II complications observed in our cohort, although this comparison should be interpreted cautiously because Benker et al. recorded complications irrespective of Clavien–Dindo grade."
}

